  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_10c18699-63a8-4727-aef9-554b952c18ad" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation that represents each graph.</para>
            <para>
              <para>
                          <media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 2 comma 2, 0 comma 0, and 2 comma 6.&lt;/p&gt;">
                    <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_01.svg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=x^2+x\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(y=x^2+x\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="b82293b7-d121-4b1a-af04-9a782684acca" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <para>
                          <media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 1, 0 comma 0, 2 comma 2.&lt;/p&gt;">
                    <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_02.svg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(y=-x^2+2\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3904b634-9a02-4278-8669-0f52e59f3a73" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <para>
                          <media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 1, 0 comma 0, 2 comma 2.&lt;/p&gt;">
                    <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_03.svg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=x^2-x\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(y=x^2-x\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ac6c8d51-6ddd-408e-8c9a-09724b084945" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <para>
                          <media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 3 comma 3, negative 1 comma negative 2, and 0 comma 0.&lt;/p&gt;">
                    <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_04.svg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(y=x^2+3x\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_47b221e5-5ecf-47ef-a87c-6397d45e81c0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Without graphing the equations, answer questions 2 – 5 to complete the table.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
              <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Equation</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(x\)-intercepts</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(x\)-coordinate of vertex</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(y=x^2+12x\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>2a. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>2b. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(y=x^2-3x\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>3a. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>3b. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(y=-x^2+16x\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>4a. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>4b. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(y=-x^2-24x\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>5a. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>5b. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\).</para>
            <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\)?
         </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.!</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \((0,0),(-12,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3dcd0fb3-d5f6-4512-87e4-078c17bde828" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -6</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_14d4938b-e405-400d-93d0-80c66d6d6c8f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\).</para>
            <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\)?
         </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \((0,0), (3,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7d788c97-8fc3-4ac2-9c93-5682536af420" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1.5</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1.5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_44d02fe9-ead0-4214-a431-386e3bc84345" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\).</para>
            <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\)?
         </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \((0,0), (16,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c941cd33-a878-4346-989f-33be17d96f3b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 8</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 8.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="daee3cea-92e3-4ed5-bfa5-8ec55ee29adb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\).</para>
            <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\)?
         </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.3 and 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is:\((0,0), (-24,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f7b96024-18a1-40e0-9882-0fa5a72c36e2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -12</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -12.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d6cf80d4-5580-4e13-8a35-00fd2bee55ae" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph that represents \(y=x^2\).

          <para>(<media alt=" &lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 8 to 8, by 2’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 12 to 12, by 2’s. A curve, labeled y equals x squared, passes through the points negative 2 comma 4, 0 comma 0, and 2 comma 4.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_05.svg"/></media>)</para>
       </para>
            <para>Describe what would happen to the graph if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\).
       </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The graph would move to the right and down.</para>
            <para>The graph would move to the left and down.</para>
            <para>The graph would move to the right and up.</para>
            <para>The graph would move to the left and up.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph would move to the right and down.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The graph would move to the right and down.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3ca7ea2c-96ba-4c27-973a-0a56c436cdc1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Predict the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(6,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(0,6)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(-6,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,0),(0,-6)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((0,0),(6,0)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is:\((0,0),(6,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_430d5ce7-c22e-4d78-bbf5-ec84afbfdc46" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>In which quadrant is the vertex located if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Fourth Quadrant</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Fourth Quadrant.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="acf83bce-9750-4918-a6ff-1dcc561f61ac" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> equations whose graph opens upward.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=(2x−1)^2\), \(y=x^2−8x−7\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are: \(y=(2x−1)^2\), \(y=x^2−8x−7\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0e291e11-6eef-42fb-9eeb-31e8067d7732" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Graph A</para>
            <para>Technology required. Use the three graphs to answer questions a–c. Then, use graphing technology to check each equation.</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. X intercepts = 0&amp;nbsp;and -7.&amp;nbsp;&quot;">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_06.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Graph B</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. Vertex = 0&amp;nbsp;comma -16.&amp;nbsp;X intercepts = -4 and 4.&amp;nbsp;&quot;">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_07.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Graph C</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. X intercepts = -9&amp;nbsp;and 3.&amp;nbsp;&quot;">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_13_08.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x(x+7)\) or \(f(x)=x^2+7x\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Graph A</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Graph A.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ed2a78ec-d3d7-400c-bbf2-5f40578b5460" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(g(x)=(x+4)(x−4)\) or \(g(x)=x^2−16\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Graph B</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Graph B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_437286ec-77dc-4d6c-a768-4e4ee1a8bd6a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(h(x)=(x−3)(x+9)\) or \(h(x)=x^2+6x−27\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Graph C</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.13.2 – 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Graph C.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--BEGIN PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="d5887686-b139-4874-86d2-d295948cb807" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation written from the real solutions \(x=-4\) and \(x=-3\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-7x+12\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+7x+12\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+7x-12\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-7x-12\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=x^2+7x+12\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y=x^2+7x+12\).</para>
            <para>\(x=-4\) and \(x=-3\) means that \(x+4=0\) and \(x+3=0\). \(y=(x+4)(x+3) = x^2+3x+4x+12 = x^2+7x+12.\)
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
    </section>
  </content>
